.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="In 1964, three independent groups showed how a gauge symmetry can be spontaneously broken while still keeping the theory&#x27;s equations exactly symmetric -- the mechanism that gives the W and Z bosons their mass while leaving the photon massless. The classical, single-field toy version is a real scalar in the double-well potential V(\phi)=-a\phi^2+b\phi^4: the field must &quot;choose&quot; one of two degenerate minima \phi=\pm v, v=\sqrt{a/(2b)}. This example evolves higgs_field_rollover from a tiny perturbation near the unstable symmetric point, watches it settle into one of the two vacua, and shows that a mirrored initial perturbation settles into the other, degenerate vacuum -- spontaneous symmetry breaking captured directly.">

