.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Glashow (1961), Weinberg (1967), and Salam (1968) showed that the electromagnetic and weak interactions are two faces of a single SU(2)\times U(1) gauge theory, spontaneously broken by the Higgs mechanism into the massless photon (electromagnetism) and the massive W^\pm,Z^0 bosons (the weak force) -- unifying two of nature&#x27;s four fundamental interactions. Gargamelle&#x27;s 1973 discovery of weak neutral currents, mediated by the previously unobserved Z^0, was the theory&#x27;s first direct experimental confirmation. This example puts the theory&#x27;s two already-modeled pieces side by side: qed_dsigma_domega_mumu, the electromagnetic sector reproduced at low energy, and higgs_vev, the symmetry-breaking scale that gives the W/Z their mass while leaving the photon massless -- exactly the two ingredients the unification joins.">

